---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import { siteStats } from '../lib/db.ts';
import KindBadge from '../components/KindBadge.astro';
import { Rss } from '../components/icons.ts';

const stats = siteStats();
---
<Base title="About" nav="about" crumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]} description="What Signal Lab is, how tools are found and how they're chosen.">
  <div class="about">
    <PageHead title="Tools worth knowing, picked by hand" />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Most developers learn about their best tools from a colleague: someone leans over and says “wait, you’re not using <em>that</em>?” Signal Lab tries to be that colleague. It is a hand-reviewed catalogue of {stats.total} developer tools, covering what’s new and the established gems that rarely get a launch post.</p>
      <p>Every entry answers the same practical questions: what it is, what it replaces, when you’d reach for it, and how to try it in a few minutes.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Two kinds of tools</h2>
    <div class="kind-compare">
      <div class="kind-box">
        <KindBadge kind="gem" />
        <p>Established, well-loved and actively maintained, yet often missed. They’ve been around long enough to prove themselves, and you’d usually hear about them from a teammate rather than a headline.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="kind-box">
        <KindBadge kind="new" />
        <p>Recently released, or picking up real momentum. Often AI-related, but not only. These are worth knowing about now, even if they’re still maturing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>How tools get here</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><span><strong>Discovery.</strong> A scanner regularly looks at rising GitHub projects, mature and actively maintained repositories across rotating topics, and well-received Show HN posts.</span></li>
      <li><span><strong>Shortlisting.</strong> An AI model reads each candidate’s README, filters out noise (tutorials, demos, abandoned projects, household names) and drafts a card for the promising ones.</span></li>
      <li><span><strong>Human review.</strong> Nothing is published automatically. Every suggestion is checked, edited or rejected by hand. Rejected tools are remembered so they don’t come back.</span></li>
      <li><span><strong>Sourced links.</strong> Website and source links come from GitHub or Hacker News data, never from the model, so it can’t invent a URL.</span></li>
      <li><span><strong>Your verdict.</strong> Every tool page asks whether you’d recommend it. No account needed, and the share of developers who would is shown on the page.</span></li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Who’s behind it</h2>
    <a class="made-by-card" href="https://dootlord.meme" rel="author">
      <img src="/dootlord.png" alt="DootLord’s avatar: a glossy black pixel-art skeleton with glowing green eyes" width="56" height="56" />
      <span><strong>Made by DootLord</strong><small>Full-stack software engineer. CV, projects and blog at dootlord.meme</small></span>
    </a>

    <h2>Follow along</h2>
    <div class="prose"><p>New tools appear as they’re reviewed. The <a href="/feed.xml">RSS feed</a> lists the 50 most recent additions, with topics as categories.</p></div>
    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:16px">
      <a class="btn" href="/feed.xml"><Rss size={16} /> Subscribe via RSS</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/browse">Browse the catalogue</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
